# Unit Cycle

## Goal

Use this skill for **exactly one task at a time**, driving it through
implement → cover → gate.

## The Law

> **Under `unit`, a behavior task is not `[x]` until a test in the *same task* covers
> that behavior and the Tier-2 gate (`npx -y @protonspy/csdd spec test-report`) has
> recorded it green.**

`unit` drops the RED-first ritual, **not the test**. What it buys is one fewer
process invocation and one fewer round-trip per behavior; what it costs is the proof
that the test could ever fail. The rest of this skill is about paying that cost
honestly instead of pretending it isn't there.

No exceptions:
- Don't ship the implementation and leave the test for "the next task".
- Don't mark the task done now and record the evidence later.
- One task per cycle — don't batch.

## When this applies

This is the **tests-after** cycle for specs whose `development_flow` is `unit`. Under
`tdd`/`tdd-e2e` the first artifact must be a failing test — use `tdd-cycle` for those
and skip this skill. In every flow, write **one test per observable behavior, not one
per code branch** — keep the test count proportional to behavior.

### When to stop and escalate to `tdd`

`unit` is the cheaper gear, not the universal one. Stop and say the flow is wrong
when the task touches:

- **money, balances, or billing** — a wrong number is expensive and often silent;
- **authentication, authorization, or tenant scoping** — a wrong boundary leaks data;
- **anything irreversible** — a migration that drops, a webhook that fires, a payout;
- **a behavior you cannot state in one sentence** — if you can't name what to observe,
  a test written after the code will just mirror the code.

Report that the task needs `tdd` rather than downgrading the coverage silently.

## Default Operation Mode

Direct implementation, tests alongside. The task is not finished when the code
works — it is finished when a test says so and the gate recorded it.

## Collect Inputs First

- Which task? (a single leaf task ID from `specs/<feature>/tasks.md`)
- What is the expected, observable behavior? **State it in one sentence before
  editing** — that sentence becomes the test's assertion.
- Which acceptance criteria does the task cite in `_Requirements:_`? Read them; they
  are the contract the test must express.
- Where do existing tests for this area live, and what is the test command?
- If the behavior calls a library/framework API, confirm that API against its
  **current** docs with the Context7 MCP tools (if connected) before writing either
  half.

## Execution Workflow

### 1) Implement the behavior
- Stay inside the task's `_Boundary:_`. No opportunistic refactors, no unrelated
  changes, no extra abstraction.
- Write the least code that delivers the stated behavior.

### 2) Cover it with tests — from the criterion, not from the code
This is the step that decides whether `unit` is honest. A test written after the
implementation drifts toward describing what the code *does*; you need one that
describes what the requirement *promised*.

- Write the assertion from the acceptance criterion's own words (the `_Requirements:_`
  IDs the task cites), not by reading back the function you just wrote.
- Assert on **observable behavior** — the returned value, the persisted row, the
  rendered state, the emitted error — never on internal call order or private shape.
- Cover the error and boundary paths the criterion names, not just the golden path.
- Match the structure and naming of the existing tests in that area.

### 3) Run the Tier-2 gate — once
One full-suite run in fast mode, recorded through csdd, is the whole task-exit gate:

```bash
npx -y @protonspy/csdd spec test-report <feature> --run --fast --lang <python|typescript|java|go|rust> --path <tests-dir>
```

- `--fast` selects the coverage-free command. Coverage belongs to Tier 3 (feat exit),
  where it is collected once instead of once per task.
- **No typecheck and no build at task exit** — those are Tier-3 checks, run once over
  the merged result by whoever owns integration.
- Then lint the touched files. That is the whole gate.
- Do not run the suite again "to be safe": this run is the evidence, and the
  `verify-change` skill's Iron Law is already satisfied by it.
- A report carrying an **open attention** is not green — resolve it or report the task
  as blocked.

### 4) Mark the task done
- Once the new test passes and the recorded run is green, edit
  `specs/<feature>/tasks.md` and change this task's checkbox from `- [ ]` to `- [x]`.
- Mark **only** the task you just completed. If your orchestrator told you it owns the
  checkboxes (concurrent dispatch), report the task green and leave `tasks.md` alone.

### 5) Update Implementation Notes and report
- Append any cross-task learning to `## Implementation Notes` in `tasks.md`.
- Report the task ID, files changed, tests added, commands run with their real
  results, and any remaining risks. Your report **is** the evidence your orchestrator
  uses — it will not re-run your checks.

## Rationalizations

Every one of these is a signal to stop, not a reason to skip. **Violating the letter of
the rule is violating its spirit.**

| Excuse | Reality |
|---|---|
| "The code is obviously correct, the test adds nothing." | `unit` already gave up the failing-test proof. Drop the test too and the task ships on nothing but your reading of it. |
| "I'll add the test in the validation phase." | Phase 4 holds end-to-end tests. A behavior's own test lives with the behavior, or it never gets written. |
| "The test passes, so the behavior works." | A test written from the code passes by construction. Write it from the criterion and it can actually disagree with you. |
| "This is money code but the task is small." | Size is not risk. Small and irreversible is the worst combination — escalate to `tdd`. |
| "I'll batch these three tasks to save time." | Batching hides which test covers which behavior. One task per cycle. |

## Red Flags — STOP

- You wrote the test by reading the implementation instead of the acceptance criterion.
- The test asserts on internal calls, mock invocation counts, or private structure.
- You cannot name, in one sentence, the behavior the test observes.
- The task touches money, auth, tenancy, or anything irreversible — it wants `tdd`.
- You're about to mark the task `[x]` with the gate unrecorded, or red, or carrying an
  open attention.
- You're about to run the full suite a second time for the same task.

If any fire, stop and return to the workflow above.

## Gotchas

- One task per invocation. Do not batch tasks "to save time".
- Never delete or weaken a test to make the suite pass.
- If no test framework exists, say so explicitly and stop — do not silently skip the test.
- Pure scaffolding (migrations, type skeletons, config) needs no behavior test; say so
  in the report rather than inventing one.
- Do not name sub-tasks `RED`/`GREEN` under this flow — `csdd spec validate` reports
  that shape as a flow mismatch, and it is telling the truth: the spec asked for `unit`.

## Verification Before Reporting

- Run: the new test, then the Tier-2 gate — once.
- Record: that run via `npx -y @protonspy/csdd spec test-report <feature> --run --fast --lang <lang>`,
  so the spec carries the evidence for this task.
- Check: the new behavior is exercised by an assertion traceable to the criterion, the
  suite is green, and the report carries no open attentions.
- If blocked: report the blocked step explicitly; do not mark the task done.

## Completion Criteria
- [ ] The behavior is covered by a passing test written in the **same task**.
- [ ] The test's assertion traces to an acceptance criterion the task cites in `_Requirements:_`.
- [ ] The Tier-2 gate passed: full suite (fast mode) + lint on the touched files.
- [ ] `npx -y @protonspy/csdd spec test-report <feature> --run --fast …` recorded the
      evidence (tests green, no open attentions).
- [ ] The completed task is marked `[x]` in `tasks.md` (only that task).
- [ ] `## Implementation Notes` updated with any cross-task learning.
- [ ] Report lists: task ID, files changed, tests added, commands run, remaining risks.
